Proposed Approach

We propose to start with a focused experiment: deploy a capable open-weight coding model on Deutsche Bank’s existing GPU infrastructure in collaboration with dbLLM, and integrate it with an open-source agent harness such as Pi or OpenCode.

The resulting agent will attempt end-to-end software engineering tasks, including bug investigation, code modification, test-data preparation, sandboxed testing, and draft pull-request generation.

We will compare its accuracy, latency, developer effort, and cost against Gemini CLI and Copilot-assisted workflows, alongside a hypothetical Claude Code subscription cost scenario.

The experiment will start with limited GPU capacity, with wider adoption considered only after validating technical performance, security, and economics.

Expected Business Outcomes

1. Reduce dependence on external paid AI intelligence

Validate whether open-weight models hosted on existing infrastructure can deliver competitive performance at a lower long-term cost for suitable workloads.

At sufficient adoption, a shared internal solution could reduce additional commercial AI subscription and API expenses. The evaluation will account for GPU opportunity cost, serving operations, maintenance, and support.

2. Strengthen data security and control

Running model inference on approved bank-controlled infrastructure can allow sensitive source code, internal documents, prompts, and generated outputs to remain within Deutsche Bank’s security boundaries rather than being transmitted to external model providers.

An internally maintained harness also enables bank-defined controls for tool permissions, execution environments, audit logging, and human oversight.

These security benefits depend on appropriate infrastructure configuration, controlled data flows, and compliance with internal security and AI governance requirements.

3. Make open-weight models widely accessible across Deutsche Bank

The coding-agent experiment is the first step toward a larger ambition: making internally hosted open-weight models a regular, practical choice for enterprise AI applications.

Working with dbLLM and Ada, we aim to establish reusable integrations that make internal models straightforward to consume without requiring each team to independently build its own access and execution framework.

Beyond coding agents, this could unlock numerous workflows involving OCR models for document processing, embedding models for enterprise search, vision models, and advanced reasoning models.

4. Maximize existing GPU investments

Demonstrate how Deutsche Bank’s existing GPU infrastructure can support reusable AI capabilities across multiple teams rather than only individual model deployment requirements.

By collaborating with dbLLM on model serving and Ada on enterprise integration, the initiative will build upon existing investments without duplicating their responsibilities.

5. Build internal AI engineering expertise

Develop in-house capabilities in open-weight model evaluation, GPU inference, agent development, context management, tool integration, performance optimization, and secure AI operations.

Over time, this expertise could strengthen Deutsche Bank’s ability to build, operate, and maintain its own AI solutions, reducing long-term reliance on external providers for both AI intelligence and implementation.

Feasibility and Success Criteria

The initial proof of concept will use limited existing GPU capacity, an approved open-source agent framework, and representative software engineering tasks in isolated environments.

Success will be evaluated through independently verified bug fixes, task completion time, developer intervention, inference resource consumption, and fully loaded cost per successful outcome.

Security validation will include approved data classification, controlled network access, credential protection, restricted tool permissions, and confirmation of applicable data residency and retention requirements.

The initiative will follow Deutsche Bank’s AI Operating Model and Path to Production process, including required software, cybersecurity, and AI governance reviews.

Long-Term Ambition

Our ultimate objective is to make internally hosted open-weight models a mainstream option for AI development across Deutsche Bank.

By proving one demanding coding-agent use case, we aim to establish the technical foundation, economic evidence, security controls, and internal expertise needed to unlock many more AI applications.

This could enable Deutsche Bank to develop increasingly powerful AI solutions on infrastructure it controls, reduce avoidable external AI expenditure, protect sensitive enterprise information, and strengthen long-term ownership of its AI technology stack.